Every endpoint is a legal state transition with guard conditions:

Endpoint	Transition	Guard
POST /subscriptions	NONE → ACTIVE	valid user/plan, authorized payment method
PATCH .../upgrade	ACTIVE → ACTIVE	must be ACTIVE, new plan must be higher tier
POST .../cancel	ACTIVE → CANCELLED	must not already be cancelled
Upgrade with Proration (the tricky math)
ini
prorated_charge = (remaining_days / total_days) × (new_price − old_price)
Why proration matters: If a user upgrades on day 20 of a 30-day cycle, they shouldn't pay the full price difference — only for the 10 remaining days of the current cycle. After upgrading, the billing cycle restarts so future invoices align cleanly.

Cancellation Strategies
Strategy	Meaning
IMMEDIATE	Features revoked now; status → CANCELLED immediately
END-OF-PERIOD	User keeps access until end_date (already paid for), then status → CANCELLED
This is a classic retention-friendly tradeoff: you honor what the customer paid for, then cut off access.

🧾 Service 3: Billing Service (Python)
Role: Invoice Production
This service constructs the bill that Payment later executes against — it separates "what to charge" from "actually charging."

The Invoice Generation Pipeline
ini
1. LOAD subscription (plan, cycle, customer)
2. GATHER line items:
   ├── base plan price
   ├── add-ons / usage charges
   └── prorated amounts (mid-cycle changes)
3. CALCULATE tax:
   └── rate = tax.get_tax_rate(country, region)   → 8%
   └── tax  = round(subtotal × rate, 2)           → $8.00
4. COMPUTE totals: subtotal = Σ(items); total = subtotal + tax
5. ASSIGN invoice number + UUID
6. SET status = "UNPAID"
7. PERSIST and RETURN
Key Separation: Billing vs. Tax
Note the architectural choice: Billing computes your charges, while tax is delegated to a separate mechanism (tax.get_tax_rate()). This hints at integrating a tax engine (e.g., VAT/GST/sales-tax providers) whose logic is jurisdiction-dependent and best isolated.

